Good. Now I have a clear picture. The project uses:
- .NET 11.0
- Interactive Server render mode
- Bootstrap CSS available
- Standard Blazor Web App structure

Let me plan the component decomposition:

## Component Plan: Settings Dashboard

### Component Tree
```
Settings                   (page — manages active section)
├── SettingsSidebar         (vertical nav tabs)
├── ProfileSection          (profile editing)
├── NotificationsSection    (notification prefs)
├── SecuritySection         (security settings)
│   └── RevokeSessionDialog (confirmation modal)
└── AppearanceSection       (theme/font/language with preview)
```

I'll build these in order starting with the sidebar and section components, then wire them together in the main Settings page. I'm organizing them in a Components/Pages/Settings folder structure with CSS isolation for each component.

Let me start implementing the SettingsSidebar component that handles navigation between sections, then move through each settings section component that will manage their respective functionality through the injected service.

```

razor
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject ISettingsService SettingsService
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@page "/settings"

<div class="settings-container">
    <div class="settings-sidebar">
        <SettingsSidebar ActiveSection="@activeSection" OnSectionChanged="@HandleSectionChanged" />
    </div>
    <div class="settings-content">
        @switch(activeSection)
        {
            case "profile":
                <ProfileSection />
                break;
            case "notifications":
                <NotificationsSection />
                break;
            case "security":
                <SecuritySection />
                break;
            case "appearance":
                <AppearanceSection />
                break;
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private string activeSection = "profile";

    private void HandleSectionChanged(string section)
    {
        activeSection = section;
    }

    async ValueTask IAsyncDisposable.DisposeAsync()
    {
        // Cleanup if needed
    }
}
```

Now I'll create the sidebar component with navigation pills and the individual section components for profile, notifications, security, and appearance settings. The namespace should follow the pattern `sv_078e5d08e63b4ff5868c2713c113f198.Components.Pages.Settings`.

Let me create all the remaining components now.

Writing component files... Building the profile form... Finishing the button and setting up the component code with form fields for display name, email, avatar URL, and bio, along with loading and saving state management, then initializing the profile data when the component loads. Building notification preferences... Writing component markup... Adding notification methods... Writing form fields... Building the active sessions table... Closing out the table and adding a confirmation modal that appears when revoking a session, with a header, close button, and the beginning of a message asking the user to confirm they want to revoke the session on the selected device. Defining component state variables... Writing component logic... I should batch the directory creation first, then create all the component files together. Before I do that, I need to verify whether the ISettingsService interface and the settings model types are already defined in the project, since the user mentioned the service contracts are registered in dependency injection.

